      ":question/problem": "If $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=-6,$ then $6\\operatorname{csc}⁡(-x-4\\pi )-4=$",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/hard",
      ":question/worked-solution": "First, let's recall the graph of $y=\\operatorname{csc}⁡x.$\n\n![](images/a8/a8e7894ea970c539ca59a73713fa15450f4361b3fc8bc22183e3fe84946411bd.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we note the following:\n\n- The period of $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x$ is $2\\pi .$\n\n- Our graph has rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin. This means that $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x$ is an *odd* function, and we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{csc}⁡(-x)=-\\operatorname{csc}⁡x.\n$$\n\nLet's now evaluate our expression:\n\n- Firstly, using the periodicity of $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{csc}⁡(-x-4\\pi ) & =\\operatorname{csc}⁡(-x-2\\pi \\cdot 2) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{csc}⁡(-x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Secondly, using the oddness of $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{csc}⁡(-x) & =-\\operatorname{csc}⁡x \\\\  & =-(-6) \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6\\operatorname{csc}⁡(-x-4\\pi )-4 & =6(6)-4 \\\\  & =36-4 \\\\  & =32.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
